WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.400
Every board asks it sooner or later.

00:00:02.675 --> 00:00:04.225
How much security is enough?

00:00:04.565 --> 00:00:07.895
Five questions turn it into
something a board can govern.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.360
First, the budget.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:16.340
The EU cybersecurity agency found that
the median organisation spent about

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:19.610
9 percent of its IT budget on security.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.170
That is a benchmark, not a target.

00:00:22.485 --> 00:00:26.055
We suggest setting the risk
appetite first, and then the budget.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.800
Second, the reporting line.

00:00:29.085 --> 00:00:31.395
Can the security lead
say no to their boss?

00:00:31.735 --> 00:00:33.665
Often they report to the CIO,

00:00:34.035 --> 00:00:37.205
who also owns the deadlines
that security can delay.

00:00:37.505 --> 00:00:42.035
We suggest a direct line to the board,
or to its audit or risk committee.

00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:45.287
Third, five numbers every quarter.

00:00:45.547 --> 00:00:47.437
Critical flaws fixed on time.

00:00:47.707 --> 00:00:49.537
Admin accounts with strong MFA.

00:00:50.002 --> 00:00:51.332
The last tested restore.

00:00:51.857 --> 00:00:54.167
Time from alert to action, out of hours.

00:00:54.527 --> 00:00:56.157
Overdue risks and findings.

00:00:56.577 --> 00:00:58.007
Each one gets a tolerance,

00:00:58.577 --> 00:00:59.367
agreed in advance.

00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:02.153
Fourth, an outside view.

00:01:02.388 --> 00:01:04.478
The team should not mark its own homework.

00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:08.223
DORA requires internal audit
for financial entities.

00:01:08.548 --> 00:01:13.178
Consider an external review after a
serious incident, or before a merger.

00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:16.693
Fifth, what the law asks
of the board itself.

00:01:17.058 --> 00:01:22.108
NIS2 Article 20 says management
bodies approve the security measures,

00:01:22.278 --> 00:01:26.048
oversee them, follow training,
and can be held liable.

00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:28.393
What good looks like.

00:01:28.678 --> 00:01:32.028
A written risk appetite,
and a budget that follows it.

00:01:32.263 --> 00:01:34.333
A security lead with a direct line.

00:01:34.658 --> 00:01:36.448
Five numbers with tolerances.

00:01:36.853 --> 00:01:38.583
Outside review at set moments.

00:01:38.948 --> 00:01:42.238
The approval is in the minutes,
and the training is on record.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.340
A suggested first step.

00:01:45.625 --> 00:01:46.915
Ask for three things.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:48.550
The written risk appetite.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.430
Proof that our security lead
can reach us without permission.

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:54.715
The minuted approval of the measures.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:00.760
Enough security is enough to stay within
the risk the board agreed to accept.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.870
Write it down, and record it.

